/**
\mainpage AMReX: Technical Reference 

  \tableofcontents

  \section welcome_to_AMReX Welcome to AMReX's Technical Reference 

  This document 
  provide searchable low-level information useful for developers and
  experienced users. 
  

  \subsection Overview

  AMReX is a software framework containing all the functionality to write 
  massively parallel, block-structured adaptive mesh refinement (AMR) 
  applications. AMReX is freely available on Github at 
  <a href="https://github.com/AMReX-Codes/amrex">AMReX-Codes/amrex</a>.

  AMReX is a project of the <a href="https://hpsf.io/">High Performance
  Software Foundation (HPSF)</a>, which is part of the nonprofit <a
  href="https://www.linuxfoundation.org/">Linux Foundation</a>.
   

  \subsection development Development

  All of AMReX’s development is done in the github repository under the 
  <a href="https://github.com/AMReX-Codes/amrex/tree/development"> 
  development branch</a>; anyone can see the latest updates. A monthly release is 
  tagged at the beginning of each month.
  

  \subsection contribute Contribute

  We are always happy to have users contribute to the AMReX source code. To 
  contribute, issue a pull request against the development branch 
  (details here). Any level of changes are welcomed: documentation, bug fixes, 
  new test problems, new solvers, etc. To obtain help, simply post a 
  <a href="https://github.com/AMReX-Codes/amrex/discussions/new">discussion</a> 
  or an 
  <a href="https://github.com/AMReX-Codes/amrex/issues/new">issue</a>
  on the AMReX GitHub webpage.
  

  \subsection tutorials Tutorials for New Users 

  There are small stand-alone example codes that demonstrate how to use 
  different parts of the AMReX functionality. The source code for these
  tutorials can be found at 
  <a href="https://github.com/AMReX-Codes/amrex-tutorials">AMReX Tutorials</a>.
  Accompanying documentation for these tutorial codes is at 
  <a href="https://amrex-codes.github.io/amrex/tutorials_html">Tutorials
  Documentation</a>.


  \subsection amrex_explained AMReX Explained

  For users who are starting out on their own development, 
  additional documentation is provided at 
  <a href="https://amrex-codes.github.io/amrex/docs_html">AMReX Source Documentation</a>. 
  In these docs you will find a thorough introduction and discussion
  of AMReX features.


  \note
  AMReX supports one-, two-, and three-dimensional applications. This
  technical reference is generated with <tt>AMREX_SPACEDIM = 3</tt>, and
  therefore for dimension-dependent types only three-dimensional
  instantiations are shown.


  \subsection api_topics API Topics

  Below is a list of major API topics:

  - \ref amrex_arithmetic_types
    This topic covers fundamental scalar arithmetic types used
    throughout AMReX, including \ref amrex::Real, \ref amrex::Long, and \ref
    amrex::GpuComplex.

  - \ref amrex_index_space
    This topic covers integer index space types, including \ref
    amrex::IndexType, \ref amrex::IntVect and \ref amrex::Box, as well as
    operations such as \ref amrex::refine and \ref amrex::coarsen.

  - \ref amrex_real_space
    This topic covers real space coordinate types and operations, including
    \ref amrex::RealVect, \ref amrex::RealBox, \ref amrex::Geometry.

  - \ref amrex_domain_decomposition
    This topic covers domain decomposition and distribution, including \ref
    amrex::BoxArray and \ref amrex::DistributionMapping.

  - \ref amrex_data_containers
    This topic covers AMReX data containers, from linear containers such as
    \ref amrex::Vector to box-based and distributed field data containers
    such as \ref amrex::MultiFab.

  - \ref amrex_execution
    This topic covers performance-portable execution and kernel launch
    interfaces including various amrex::ParallelFor functions.

  - \ref amrex_collectives
    This topic covers performance-portable reductions and scans, including
    \ref amrex::Reduce::Sum, \ref amrex::Scan::PrefixSum.

  - \ref amrex_mpi
    This topic covers MPI based communication capabilities in AMReX.

  - \ref amrex_amr
    This topic covers adaptive mesh refine (AMR) interfaces, including both
    \ref amrex_amrcore and \ref amrex_amrlevel approaches.

  - \ref amrex_linear_solvers
    This topic covers linear system solver interfaces in AMReX, including
    \ref amrex::MLMG and \ref amrex::GMRES.

  - \ref amrex_particles
    This topic covers particle data structures in AMReX.

  - \ref amrex_eb
    This topic covers the embedded boundary capabilities in AMReX, including
    geometry generation, EB data structures and solvers

  - \ref amrex_fft
    This topic covers parallel Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) interfaces in
    AMReX, including FFT operations and FFT-based solvers.

  - \ref amrex_io
    This topic covers I/O interfaces in AMReX.

*/

